## Summary

Build a **development-only upstream and community intelligence pipeline** for TeknoParrot Manager compatibility maintenance.

The preferred implementation is a GitHub Actions workflow plus repository-owned development tooling. It monitors approved sources, preserves provenance, detects meaningful changes, correlates evidence, and creates reviewable engineering outputs such as GitHub issues, reports, data-change proposals, or pull requests.

This is development infrastructure, similar in deployment boundary to the TPM certification/test engine. It is not an end-user TPM runtime feature and is never shipped as autonomous monitoring software on users' cabinets.

## Product decision

- Upstream and community monitoring belongs in the development workflow, not inside installed copies of TPM.
- GitHub Actions is the preferred execution environment.
- Findings inform maintainers; they do not directly change users' code or configuration.
- Every accepted compatibility change follows normal review, testing, documentation, and release gates.
- TPM must never rewrite its own executable code based on a monitoring finding.
- User installations must not crawl repositories, forums, DAT sites, asset sites, or community pages.
- No background monitor, startup scan, local intelligence database, or silent scheduled task is installed on a user's machine.
- Routine monitoring must not require an AI service.
- AI may assist maintainers with a reviewed finding, but it is not part of the production pipeline or its authority model.
- Community claims remain evidence leads until corroborated; they are never promoted automatically to authoritative facts.

## Explicitly out of scope

This issue does not authorize TPM on a user's PC to:

- monitor internet sources at startup;
- run daily or weekly background checks;
- create a Windows Scheduled Task;
- maintain a local SQLite intelligence database;
- scrape public or authenticated community accounts;
- download or execute discovered code;
- modify active compatibility data automatically;
- install modules, providers, patches, firmware, or third-party tools;
- rewrite `TeknoParrot-Manager.ps1` or any other executable component;
- silently change GameProfiles, UserProfiles, game files, configuration, or cabinet settings;
- notify users about raw development findings.

Any future proposal for user-initiated compatibility-data updates requires a separate issue and explicit product decision. Such a proposal must use one controlled Jumpstile endpoint, signed/versioned artifacts, schema validation, rollback, clear consent, and an absolute prohibition on executable-code mutation.

## Initial monitored sources

### Official TeknoParrot ecosystem

Monitor relevant official repositories and release channels, including at minimum:

- TeknoParrotUI releases and GameProfiles;
- GameProfile/schema changes and `GameProfileRevision` values;
- ParrotPatcher and updater components;
- official launcher/runtime/support repositories;
- official platform integrations, plugins, utilities, and compatibility data.

### Eggman sources

- `https://github.com/Eggmansworld/TeknoParrot`
- `https://github.com/Eggmansworld/Datfiles`

Monitor commits, releases, tags, relevant DAT files, repository structure, metadata, and tooling changes.

### Approved public community knowledge

Approved public sources may be monitored read-only for evidence leads, beginning with:

- `https://www.retroroms.info/view-discussion/teknoparrot-roms`
- Eggman's Game Notes & Compatibility Reference, with commit provenance preserved

Community monitoring must respect access controls, robots guidance, source terms, licensing, creator rights, and rate limits. Never use personal-account tokens, self-bots, private content, or access-control bypasses.

## Watch areas

- New, renamed, removed, or changed games
- GameProfile additions, removals, and revisions
- Profile/schema drift
- DAT changes, aliases, bad dumps, and identity convergence
- Runtime/platform changes, including PCSX2x6, CXBXR, ElfLdr2, RPCS3, BudgieLoader, and future runtimes
- TeknoParrotUI installation, update, and layout changes
- FFB Blaster and third-party FFB compatibility
- Hook Of The Reaper, RawInput, lightgun, trackball, wheel, and controller behavior
- ReShade, dgVoodoo2, GPU fixes, patches, and setup utilities
- Deprecated fields, removed capabilities, and breaking changes
- Optional artwork, bezels, marquees, videos, manuals, shaders, and other enhancements
- Recurring support patterns suitable for TPM diagnostics, documentation, or future reviewed automation

## Architecture

### Execution boundary

Run only in repository-controlled development infrastructure:

- scheduled GitHub Actions workflow;
- manual `workflow_dispatch` run;
- optional maintainer-local development command using the same checked-in engine;
- no execution from an installed TPM release.

### Source adapters

Use a checked-in, reviewable source manifest. Each adapter defines:

- source identity and trust classification;
- approved endpoints and paths;
- revision detection strategy;
- expected schemas and file patterns;
- parsing and semantic comparison rules;
- legal, licensing, access, and redistribution constraints;
- rate limits, timeouts, and maximum response sizes;
- notification and escalation policy.

Adding or changing a monitored source requires a reviewed repository change. A remotely supplied source list must never grant itself authority.

### State

Prefer repository-safe GitHub infrastructure rather than a database on users' machines. Candidate state mechanisms include:

- workflow artifacts with bounded retention;
- GitHub cache for non-authoritative acceleration only;
- checked-in, reviewed revision manifests where durable state is required;
- issue/PR labels and machine-readable report attachments;
- a dedicated development-side datastore only if later justified and separately reviewed.

Caches are never authority. Every accepted fact retains its source revision and can be independently reconstructed.

### Outputs

The pipeline may create or update:

- structured workflow artifacts;
- a development report attached to a workflow run;
- deduplicated GitHub issues for actionable findings;
- comments on an existing tracking issue when appropriate;
- proposed compatibility-data changes on a bot branch;
- draft pull requests containing reviewable data/documentation/test changes.

It must not automatically merge a pull request, advance a release branch, publish a release, modify a user's installation, or activate a finding in production.

## Evidence and confidence model

Every finding distinguishes:

- directly observed source facts;
- source-author claims;
- repeated community reports;
- pipeline-generated inference;
- official corroboration;
- reproducible local evidence;
- human review and acceptance.

Suggested confidence states:

- **Confirmed** — official source or reproducible local evidence
- **High confidence** — multiple independent sources including a trusted source
- **Medium confidence** — repeated/plausible community evidence without authoritative confirmation
- **Low confidence** — single unverified report or ambiguous mapping
- **Rejected/obsolete** — disproven, superseded, or no longer applicable

Confidence affects triage priority and proposed action only. It never grants automatic merge, release, code execution, or user-machine mutation authority.

## Change detection

Compare substantive identity and content rather than timestamps alone:

- commit SHA;
- release/tag version;
- content SHA-256;
- parsed semantic content;
- schema version;
- `GameProfileRevision`;
- normalized entity identity;
- source-page fingerprint where a repository revision is unavailable.

Classify additions, removals, renames, aliases, metadata-only changes, schema changes, stale profiles, runtime changes, optional enhancements, recurring support patterns, conflicting evidence, and possible breaking changes.

Correlate related observations into one reviewed event. For example, a new official GameProfile, matching Eggman DAT entry, and community setup notes should become one new-game onboarding finding with separate provenance for each source.

## GitHub Actions behavior

Support:

- scheduled runs at a conservative frequency;
- manual `workflow_dispatch` runs;
- concurrency control preventing overlapping monitors;
- deterministic no-change behavior;
- deduplication so the same revision cannot repeatedly create issues;
- bounded artifacts, logs, downloads, and execution time;
- least-privilege `GITHUB_TOKEN` permissions;
- no repository mutation during untrusted parsing;
- a separate, explicit reviewed step for creating an issue or draft PR;
- a dry-run mode that produces artifacts without GitHub mutations.

No-change runs should update only development-side workflow state and produce no user-facing noise.

## Security, legal, and safety

- HTTPS only
- Explicit domain/repository allowlist
- Pin third-party GitHub Actions to immutable commit SHAs
- Least-privilege workflow permissions
- No `pull_request_target` execution of untrusted contribution code
- Strict timeouts and response-size limits
- Safe XML/JSON/HTML parsing with external entities disabled
- Schema validation before semantic processing
- No downloaded-code execution
- No dynamic `Invoke-Expression`, shell evaluation, or source-controlled command construction from remote values
- No secrets exposed to parsing steps
- Complete source URL, commit/release identity, timestamp, and SHA-256 provenance
- License and redistribution status retained for assets, patches, and metadata
- Never download or redistribute copyrighted game content
- Atomic generation of proposed artifacts
- Human review required before repository data changes are accepted
- Existing branch protections, tests, independent review, and release certification remain mandatory

## Tests

Add automated coverage for:

- source-adapter contract validation;
- allowlist enforcement;
- same-commit/same-hash no-op behavior;
- semantic diffing and alias handling;
- source correlation without provenance collapse;
- confidence classification;
- malformed and hostile XML/JSON/HTML;
- timeout and size-limit behavior;
- parser/schema drift;
- deterministic repeat runs;
- deduplicated issue/PR proposals;
- dry-run behavior;
- offline fixture-based test execution;
- no automatic merge or release operation;
- no executable-code generation or activation;
- no dependency on a user's TPM installation;
- no network calls from the released TPM runtime.

All routine tests should use checked-in fixtures. Live-source smoke checks belong in a separately controlled workflow and must not make repository changes.

## Documentation

Document:

- development-only deployment boundary;
- monitored sources and trust classifications;
- source-adapter review process;
- workflow schedule and manual trigger;
- permissions and secrets;
- state, artifact retention, and deduplication;
- evidence/confidence semantics;
- issue and draft-PR output behavior;
- dry-run and local development modes;
- recovery from parser/source drift;
- how maintainers accept, reject, or supersede findings;
- confirmation that installed TPM copies do not perform this monitoring.

## Relationship to other issues

- Parent epic: #49
- Automated new-game onboarding: #44
- Schema drift tests: #43
- Compatibility regression coverage: #46
- Release safety review: #47
- Professional GUI roadmap: #117
- Integrated Patch Management System: #143
- TeknoParrotUI .NET 8 investigations: #141 and #142
- Golden Tee trackball setup research: #196

## Acceptance criteria

- Monitoring runs only in development-controlled infrastructure.
- A scheduled and manually dispatched GitHub Actions workflow can inspect approved sources using least privilege.
- Installed TPM copies perform no monitoring or broad internet crawling for this feature.
- The pipeline preserves source identity, revisions, timestamps, hashes, trust classification, and corroboration.
- Unchanged sources produce no duplicate issue or pull-request noise.
- Community evidence cannot become an accepted compatibility fact without corroboration and review.
- Findings produce bounded development artifacts, deduplicated issues, or draft pull requests.
- No finding can automatically merge, publish, execute downloaded code, rewrite TPM, or modify a user's installation.
- Every accepted compatibility change passes normal code review, tests, documentation synchronization, and release gates.
- The system can be disabled without affecting TPM runtime behavior.
- Security and failure-mode tests prove untrusted source content cannot escape the parser/output boundary.

## Release scope

Post-1.0 development infrastructure. It must remain operationally separate from the shipped TPM runtime and must not delay or alter the active PR #155 certification sequence.
